1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529
Every company knows more than any one person can find.

2
00:00:03,709 --> 00:00:06,547
Know-how sits on a few laptops and in a few heads.

3
00:00:06,727 --> 00:00:09,009
Data the company paid for goes unopened.

4
00:00:09,189 --> 00:00:10,832
Our team has seen this firsthand.

5
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:15,129
Atlassian found that teams lose a quarter of their time searching for answers.

6
00:00:15,309 --> 00:00:16,995
Kohu is one place to ask.

7
00:00:18,075 --> 00:00:20,955
A new hire asks how to process a customer refund.

8
00:00:21,135 --> 00:00:26,937
Muse Spark searches only the sources this role may see, and every sentence comes back with a citation.

9
00:00:27,117 --> 00:00:28,973
This one opens the written process.

10
00:00:29,153 --> 00:00:35,639
This one plays the screen recording from the exact second, and SAM 3.1 marks the field to use.

11
00:00:35,819 --> 00:00:41,195
Kohu built these steps from the recording itself, with Muse Voice Transcribe and Muse Spark.

12
00:00:41,375 --> 00:00:43,871
A reason is kept only when the narrator said it.

13
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:48,172
Next the new hire asks which country grew fastest last year.

14
00:00:48,352 --> 00:00:53,621
The answer comes from a report the company licensed for everyone, one most people never knew it had.

15
00:00:53,801 --> 00:00:57,236
SAM marks the chart, and Muse Spark reads the values.

16
00:00:58,316 --> 00:01:01,580
The full dataset behind that report has only a few seats.

17
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:07,413
Asked by all four roles, only the analyst gets the exact value, cited to the row and cell.

18
00:01:07,593 --> 00:01:10,708
The others get nothing from it, and no hint that it exists.

19
00:01:11,788 --> 00:01:13,409
Here two sources disagree.

20
00:01:13,589 --> 00:01:18,368
The licensed report says Viet Nam grew five percent in 2023.

21
00:01:18,548 --> 00:01:20,532
An internal slide says six and a half.

22
00:01:20,712 --> 00:01:22,611
Kohu shows both and does not pick.

23
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:26,311
The steward, the data admin, decides that the report is right.

24
00:01:26,491 --> 00:01:28,453
Ask again, and the answer changes.

25
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:30,553
The slide is marked as superseded.

26
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:33,724
A manager uploads a new recording.

27
00:01:33,904 --> 00:01:39,643
Kohu transcribes it, sends the video and the transcript to Muse Spark, and gets back timed steps.

28
00:01:39,823 --> 00:01:41,295
The waiting is sped up here.

29
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:46,573
Each step gets a frame, a mark from SAM, and a reason only if one was spoken.

30
00:01:46,753 --> 00:01:49,313
A person approves it before anyone can cite it.

31
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:52,932
The license card shows who used the paid data.

32
00:01:53,112 --> 00:01:55,288
The baseline is labeled sample data.

33
00:01:56,368 --> 00:01:57,243
We measured it.

34
00:01:57,423 --> 00:02:04,143
Nineteen or twenty of twenty frozen questions passed in each run, with no leaks in seventy four access checks.

35
00:02:04,323 --> 00:02:06,136
The failures are in the repo too.

